
  [1mB. Materialized fan-out — one notification row per recipient[0m
  On each mutation, compute the recipient set and insert a row per person: [38;5;153mnotifications (user_id, task_id, event_kind,
[39m  [38;5;153mread, at)[39m.
  [3mBuys:[0m trivial read path ([38;5;153mWHERE user_id = ? AND read = false[39m), read state is just a column, and recipients are frozen
  at the moment it happened — which matches most people's intuition.
  [3mCosts:[0m write amplification, and recipient logic sits on the write path where a bug silently drops notifications.
  You'll still want an events table for audit, so this tends to become two tables anyway. Rule changes don't apply
  retroactively.
  [3mRules out:[0m little, but it's the harder one to walk back, because the rows are already written.

  [1mC. Hybrid — events table plus fan-out rows referencing it[0m
  What GitHub-scale systems converge on: log for truth, materialized rows for delivery.
  [3mBuys:[0m both sets of advantages, and each notification row is already a delivery job when email or push arrives.
  [3mCosts:[0m two tables, two code paths, and a consistency question between them, bought entirely for channels you've said
  you don't want.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m In-app-only delivery and implicit involvement are exactly the conditions where the derived
  approach wins — the reasons to materialize are outbound delivery and huge fan-out, and you have neither. It's also the
  smallest thing that still gives you the audit trail, which for a coordination tool you'll want regardless. If email
  ever lands, A becomes C by adding a table, not by rewriting one.
